          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Transition and main-group elements can form coordination compounds, where a central metal atom or ion bonds to one or more ligands via coordinate covalent bonds. Ligands with multiple donor atoms, known as polydentate ligands, form chelates. Common geometries in these complexes include tetrahedral, square planar, and octahedral."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3255842229",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Geometric isomers, such as "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "cis"
              },
              {
                "text": "- and "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "trans"
              },
              {
                "text": "- forms, can occur in some complexes, while certain octahedral complexes can also have optical isomers, which are mirror images that cannot be superimposed. Coordination complexes play essential roles in various applications, including oxygen transport in blood (hemoglobin), water purification, and pharmaceuticals."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1658651010",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
